      en: "The Kamina Radio Station Ruins in Togo are the remains of a powerful long-wave radio transmitter built by Germany in 1914. It was one of the most advanced wireless stations of its time, enabling communication between the German colonial empire and its ships. During World War I, the station was destroyed by French forces to neutralize German communications. Today, the site features concrete foundations, antenna bases, and building walls. It stands as a historical landmark documenting early wireless technology and colonial competition in Africa.",
